sallie Challen is still adapting to her  free life not only is she out of prison  but for the first time she's free to  decide how she lives mental abuse is the  most difficult thing to explain to  people I think that over the years mine  was like a drip feed coming at me and it  came to a crescendo at the end it's  something that makes you question your  own mind  we should did rape me he did drag me  down the stairs and throw me out of his  flat once you don't know how to get out  of it because you're so conditioned to  being with that person you can't see a  life without them sally met her husband  Richard when she was just 15  they married had two sons and were  together 40 years the outside world a  happy family what people didn't see was  that sally was a victim of sustained  psychological abuse a crime now  recognized as coercive control and in  August 2010 after suspecting Richard  have yet another affair she snapped I  gave him his food and I said to him am I  going to see you tomorrow and he said  don't question me don't question me and  I grabbed a hammer that was in my bag  and hit him they say I hit him 18 times  I can't think that I could possibly have  been capable of doing something  something he just snapped inside me I  admit that the hammer was in my bag but  I've got no recollection of putting it  in there  I then covered him up and wrote a note  saying I love you um I put it on his  body I went home and then the next day I  went to beat she head where I was  planning to jump and it was only the  police negotiator who talked me through  everything and mentioned about how my  sons would be on their own which made me  come back from behind  the fence do you regret what you did oh  god yes I wish I could turn the clock  back he didn't deserve to die and I'm  really sorry for what happened Sally was  sentenced to life for killing Richard a  trial she barely remembers by the time  my trial had come up I dropped  considerably mentally and it was just a  blur all I can really remember is being  asked questions and answering I suppose  so  I suppose so I think the main thing that  I remember is my eldest son screaming at  my sentence I thought I'd die in prison  I didn't think there was any hope but  hope came when coercive control was made  a crime while sally was behind bars it  allowed her lawyers to fight to change  her case to manslaughter ultimately  leading to her release it was surreal  really walking out of there but I want  to help those that are still there who  shouldn't be there they need a voice and  I think I have to be that voice for them  Sally wants to use her story to educate  others about coercive control and  highlight the importance of a proposed  new law on domestic violence it has to  happen otherwise you can have the  situation where you have more women in  prison for killing they're pushed to the  brink and it's the only way they can see  that they can get out it was right that  I went to prison but i reaiiy think the  charge and the conviction was wrong now  that I say that I've come out the other  side I want to help other people fight  for justice  Charlotte Lomas Sky News
 

